The problem is kind of simple that it even fits here:

We are given integers $x < y$ (up to $10^{16}$). A **friend group of $x$ and $y$** is a strictly increasing integer sequence

```math
x = a_0 < a_1 < a_2 < \cdots < a_k = y
```

such that for each consecutive pair, $|a_{i+1} - a_i|$ is a decimal digit of $a_i$ or of $a_{i+1}$. We must count such sequences modulo $998244353$.

## Naive DP

Since decimal digits live in $\{0,\dots,9\}$, consecutive elements differ by at most $9$. Let

```math
\mathrm{dp}[n]=\#\{\text{friend groups of }x\text{ and }n\text{ ending at }n\}.
```

Base: $\mathrm{dp}[x]=1$, and $\mathrm{dp}[n]=0$ for $n<x$. Perform push DP: processing sources $i$ in increasing order, for $i\ge x$ and $d\in\{1,\dots,9\}$,

```math
\mathrm{dp}[i+d]\mathrel{+}=\mathrm{dp}[i]
```

whenever $d\in D(i)\cup D(i+d)$,

where $D(n)$ is the set of **distinct** decimal digits appearing in the usual decimal representation of $n$ (no leading zeros).

The implementation is straightforward, but since this is a Kevin problem, it will TLE.

## Matrix representation of a DP transition

Define a $9$-element state vector

```math
S_n=
\begin{pmatrix}
s_0\\
s_1\\
\vdots\\
s_8
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathrm{dp}[n+0]\\
\mathrm{dp}[n+1]\\
\vdots\\
\mathrm{dp}[n+8]
\end{pmatrix}.
```

At $S_n$, the transition to $S_{n+1}$ is:

* For $0\le i\le 7$, the value $\mathrm{dp}[n+1+i]$ shifts into position $i$. Thus

  ```math
  M_n[i][i+1]=1.
  ```

* The value $\mathrm{dp}[n]$ can jump by $d\in\{1,\dots,9\}$, landing at $n+d$. In the next state that is position $d-1$. The jump is allowed if

  ```math
  d\in D(n)\quad\text{or}\quad d\in D(n+d).
  ```

  Thus

  ```math
  M_n[d-1][0]=1
  ```

  for each allowed $d$.

So $S_{n+1}=M_nS_n$.

One subtlety: position $8$ of $S_{n+1}$ holds $\mathrm{dp}[n+9]$, and at this moment it receives only the $d=9$ push from $\mathrm{dp}[n]$. This is intentional: each source pushes exactly once, when it sits at position $0$ and its value is final. The remaining contributions to $\mathrm{dp}[n+9]$ (pushes of sizes $8,7,\dots,1$ from $\mathrm{dp}[n+1],\dots,\mathrm{dp}[n+8]$) are added later, by $M_{n+1},\dots,M_{n+8}$, as those values reach position $0$. This is exactly the behavior of the naive DP with sources processed in increasing order.

The issue is that $M_n$ depends on $n$, so we cannot immediately use matrix exponentiation.

## Making blocks of transitions

Consider a block of transitions such as

```math
S_{12345678000}\to S_{12345678001}\to\cdots\to S_{12345678991}.
```

Write out the state vectors:

```math
S_{12345678000}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathrm{dp}[12345678000]\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345678001]\\
\vdots\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345678008]
\end{pmatrix},
```

```math
S_{12345678001}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathrm{dp}[12345678001]\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345678002]\\
\vdots\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345678009]
\end{pmatrix},
```

```math
\cdots
```

```math
S_{12345678991}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathrm{dp}[12345678991]\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345678992]\\
\vdots\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345678999]
\end{pmatrix}.
```

Within this block, only the last three decimal digits of the index vary. Every index appearing in the block has a common high part

```math
H=12345678.
```

Notice that the last state we use is $S_{12345678991}$, not $S_{12345678992}$. If we took one more step to $S_{12345678992}$, the window would be

```math
S_{12345678992}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathrm{dp}[12345678992]\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345678993]\\
\vdots\\
\mathrm{dp}[12345679000]
\end{pmatrix},
```

which has $\mathrm{dp}$ indices whose high part is $12345679$, not $12345678$. That is why we stop nine early: the state is a sliding window of length $9$, so we must stop after $10^k-9$ iterations of transitions, not $10^k-1$.

This is why the number $10^k-9$ appears everywhere.

### Comparing blocks with different high parts

Recall the transition matrix $M_n$ uses two pieces of information:

* $D(n)$, the set of decimal digits appearing in $n$;
* $D(n+d)$ for $d\in\{1,\dots,9\}$.

In both cases, the high part $H$ contributes only through the **set** $D(H)$. The order of the digits of $H$ is irrelevant.

The reason is simply that the jump condition is $d\in D(n)\cup D(n+d),$ which depends only on whether a digit is present, not on where it appears.

Consider another block obtained by permuting the high digits of an existing block:

```math
S_{87654321000}\to S_{87654321001}\to\cdots\to S_{87654321991}.
```

Here the common high part is $H'=87654321$.

The digit sets agree:

```math
D(12345678)=D(87654321)=\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\}.
```

More precisely, for every transition offset $0\le \ell\le 990$ (i.e. every transition with source $12345678000+\ell$),

```math
M_{12345678000+\ell}=M_{87654321000+\ell}.
```

The whole block $[12345678000, 12345679000)$ has exactly the same transition matrices as the block $[87654321000, 87654322000)$. For blocks with the same digit set, we only need to compute their transition matrices once.

### How many different blocks do we need?

A digit set is a subset of $\{0,1,\dots,9\}$, so there are at most

```math
2^{10}=1024
```

possible high-digit sets. Therefore we can precompute one lifted transition for each pair $(k,F)$, where $k$ is the block level and $F$ is the set of digits in the fixed high part.

## Solving with a lifting-like algorithm

We use a technique inspired by doubling (ダブリング), also known as binary lifting in English, but we do decimal lifting instead.

Let $H$ be the high part of a number, and let $F$ be the set of decimal digits appearing in $H$. Consider the block beginning at $L = H \cdot 10^k$, whose last $k \ge 1$ digits are all zero.

Define $\operatorname{Lift}(k,F)$ to apply the first $10^k - 9$ transitions within this block. In other words, it maps

```math
  
S_L \longmapsto S_{L + 10^k - 9}.  
  
```

For example, when $k = 2$, the lift maps $S_{L+0}$ to $S_{L+91}$, using the transitions with source indices $L+0, L+1, \ldots, L+90$. For $k=2$, the corresponding mapping for a block beginning at $L$ is

```math
  
S_L \longmapsto S_{L+91}.  
  
```

We still intentionally omit the final nine transitions, to keep the high part consistent across all $S_n$.

### Base case

For $k=1$, only the transition at offset $0$ is safe, since $10^1-9=1$. $\operatorname{Lift}(1,F)$ is just the single transition matrix $M_{H\cdot 10}$ that does

```math
  
S_{H\cdot 10^1} \longmapsto S_{H\cdot 10^1+1}.  
  
```

### Recursive construction

To build $\operatorname{Lift}(k,F)$, split the transition offsets $[0,10^k-9)$ of the block into ten runs according to the most significant decimal digit $d=0,1,\dots,9$ of the offset. For example, if we have $k=3$, we end up with the subblocks $[0, 100), \dots, [800, 900), [900, 991)$.

For $d=0,1,\dots,8$:

* Use $\operatorname{Lift}(k-1,F\cup\{d\})$ to cover the first $10^{k-1}-9$ transitions of that subblock.
* Then naively perform the next $9$ transitions to reach the start of the following subblock.

For $d=9$:

* Use only $\operatorname{Lift}(k-1,F\cup\{9\})$ and stop there.
* The nine transitions it omits are precisely the last nine transitions of the whole block (offsets $10^k-9,\dots,10^k-1$), i.e. exactly the ones $\operatorname{Lift}(k,F)$ wants to omit, so we do not add them back.

Thus each subblock is "big precomputed piece plus nine naive steps," except the final subblock, which is "big precomputed piece only."

### Performing lifting for nearly-arbitrary power of 10-length blocks

To move the state across a whole block $[L,L+10^k)$, where $L$ is a multiple of $10^k$ (i.e. the lowest $k$ decimal digits of $L$ are zero):

1. Let

   ```math
   F = D\!\left(\frac{L}{10^k}\right)
   ```

   be the digit set of the high part that is common to all numbers in the block.
2. Apply $\operatorname{Lift}(k,F)$. This carries the state from $L$ to $L+10^k-9$.
3. Naively apply the last nine transitions at

   ```math
   n = L+10^k-9,\dots,L+10^k-1.
   ```

Together these cover the whole block $[L,L+10^k)$. We illustrate how to apply this for general $a$ and $b$ with an example.

## Example: going from 67 to 2026

Start at $a=67$ with the state $S_{67}$, where only $\mathrm{dp}[67]=1$. Repeatedly choose the largest power of ten dividing $a$ (if $a=0$, use $10^0=1$); if it causes us to overshoot $b$, try a smaller power of ten. A block of length $1$ is simply a single naive transition.

Grouping consecutive steps by pattern gives:

| stage | range         | decomposition               | high-digit sets     | notes                                                                                                       |
| ----- | ------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| A     | $67\to70$     | three unit steps            | —                   | naive transitions at $67,68,69$                                                                             |
| B     | $70\to100$    | three blocks of length $10$ | $\{7\},\{8\},\{9\}$ | for $d=7,8,9$: apply $\operatorname{Lift}(1,\{d\})$, then $9$ naive transitions                             |
| C     | $100\to1000$  | nine blocks of length $100$ | $\{1\},\dots,\{9\}$ | for $d=1,\dots,9$: apply $\operatorname{Lift}(2,\{d\})$, then $9$ naive transitions                         |
| D     | $1000\to2000$ | one block of length $1000$  | $\{1\}$             | apply $\operatorname{Lift}(3,\{1\})$, then $9$ naive transitions                                            |
| E     | $2000\to2020$ | two blocks of length $10$   | $\{2,0\},\{2,0,1\}$ | apply $\operatorname{Lift}(1,\{2,0\})$, then $9$ naive; then $\operatorname{Lift}(1,\{2,0,1\})$, then $9$ naive |
| F     | $2020\to2026$ | six unit steps              | —                   | naive transitions at $2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025$                                                        |

At $a=2000$, the largest power of ten dividing $2000$ is $1000$, but $1000 > 2026-2000 = 26$, so it is shrunk to $10$. That is why the block $[2000,2010)$ uses length $10$, not $1000$.

For a block $[a,a+10^k)$, the set $F$ is the set of digits in the high part that is common to all numbers in the range:

```math
F = D\!\left(\frac{a}{10^k}\right).
```

For example:

```math
[70,80):\quad F = D(7)=\{7\},
```

```math
[100,200):\quad F = D(1)=\{1\},
```

```math
[2000,2010):\quad F = D(200)=\{2,0\},
```

```math
[2010,2020):\quad F = D(201)=\{2,0,1\}.
```

After the final block $[2025,2026)$, the maintained state is $S_{2026}$, and its first coordinate is $\mathrm{dp}[2026]$, the answer.

<details>
    <summary>C++ code</summary>

```c++
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

template <int MOD>
struct Fp {
    int64_t val = 0;

    constexpr Fp(int64_t v = 0) noexcept : val(v % MOD) {
        if (val < 0) val += MOD;
    }

    constexpr Fp operator-() const noexcept {
        return val ? MOD - val : 0;
    }

    constexpr Fp operator+(const Fp& r) const noexcept { return Fp(*this) += r; }
    constexpr Fp operator-(const Fp& r) const noexcept { return Fp(*this) -= r; }
    constexpr Fp operator*(const Fp& r) const noexcept { return Fp(*this) *= r; }
    constexpr Fp operator/(const Fp& r) const noexcept { return Fp(*this) /= r; }

    constexpr Fp& operator+=(const Fp& r) noexcept {
        val += r.val;
        if (val >= MOD) val -= MOD;
        return *this;
    }

    constexpr Fp& operator-=(const Fp& r) noexcept {
        val -= r.val;
        if (val < 0) val += MOD;
        return *this;
    }

    constexpr Fp& operator*=(const Fp& r) noexcept {
        val = val * r.val % MOD;
        return *this;
    }

    constexpr Fp& operator/=(const Fp& r) noexcept {
        int64_t a = r.val, b = MOD, u = 1, v = 0;
        while (b) {
            int64_t t = a / b;
            a -= t * b;
            swap(a, b);
            u -= t * v;
            swap(u, v);
        }
        val = val * u % MOD;
        if (val < 0) val += MOD;
        return *this;
    }

    constexpr bool operator==(const Fp& r) const noexcept {
        return val == r.val;
    }

    constexpr bool operator!=(const Fp& r) const noexcept {
        return val != r.val;
    }

    friend constexpr ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Fp<MOD>& x) noexcept {
        return os << x.val;
    }
};

const int MOD = 998244353;
using mint = Fp<MOD>;

long long place_value_first_non0(long long n) {
    if (n == 0) return 1;

    long long ret = 1;
    while (n % 10 == 0) {
        n /= 10;
        ret *= 10;
    }
    return ret;
}

long long n_to_mask(long long n) {
    long long ret = 0;
    while (n > 0) {
        int d = n % 10;
        ret |= 1LL << d;
        n /= 10;
    }
    return ret;
}

using state = array<mint, 9>;
using matrix = array<array<mint, 9>, 9>;

matrix identity() {
    matrix ret = {};
    for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
        ret[i][i] = 1;
    }
    return ret;
}

state matmul(const matrix& m, state s) {
    state ret = {};

    for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 9; j++) {
            ret[i] += m[i][j] * s[j];
        }
    }

    return ret;
}

matrix matmul(const matrix& a, const matrix& b) {
    matrix ret = {};

    for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 9; j++) {
            for (int k = 0; k < 9; k++) {
                ret[i][j] += a[i][k] * b[k][j];
            }
        }
    }

    return ret;
}

matrix gen(long long base, long long force_allow_digits = 0) {
    matrix ret = {};

    for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
        ret[i][i + 1] = 1;
    }

    long long digits_allowed = force_allow_digits;
    {
        digits_allowed |= n_to_mask(base);

        for (int d = 1; d < 10; d++) {
            if (n_to_mask(base + d) & (1LL << d)) {
                digits_allowed |= 1LL << d;
            }
        }
    }

    for (int d = 1; d < 10; d++) {
        if ((1LL << d) & digits_allowed) {
            ret[d - 1][0] += 1;
        }
    }

    return ret;
}

array<unordered_map<long long, matrix>, (1 << 10)> cache;

matrix get_full(long long width, long long force_allow_digits = 0) {
    assert(width >= 10);

    if (cache[force_allow_digits].contains(width)) {
        return cache[force_allow_digits][width];
    }

    if (width == 10) {
        return cache[force_allow_digits][width] = gen(0, force_allow_digits);
    }

    auto ret = identity();

    for (int i = 0; i <= 9; i++) {
        ret = matmul(get_full(width / 10, force_allow_digits | (1LL << i)), ret);

        if (i <= 8) {
            for (int j = 9; j >= 1; j--) {
                ret = matmul(gen(width / 10 * (i + 1) - j, force_allow_digits), ret);
            }
        }
    }

    cache[force_allow_digits][width] = ret;
    return ret;
}

matrix get(long long width, long long l) {
    if (width == 1) {
        return gen(l);
    }

    auto ret = get_full(width, n_to_mask(l));

    for (int j = 9; j >= 1; j--) {
        ret = matmul(gen(l + width - j), ret);
    }

    return ret;
}

state transform(long long width, long long l, state cur) {
    auto m = get(width, l);
    return matmul(m, cur);
}

void solve() {
    long long a, b;
    cin >> a >> b;

    state dp = {};
    dp[0] = 1;

    while (b - a > 0) {
        long long pv = place_value_first_non0(a);
        while (pv > b - a) {
            pv /= 10;
        }

        dp = transform(pv, a, dp);
        a += pv;
    }

    cout << dp[0] << '\n';
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(NULL);

    int t = 1;
    while (t--) solve();
}
```

</details>